course: dictionary
lesson: 4269
title: Gravity: the invisible pull
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Gravity is a force that pulls things towards each other. On Earth, it is the pull that makes things fall and gives them weight. When you drop a ball, gravity pulls it down, and it does not stop pulling until the ball lands.
explain: Gravity also keeps the Moon going round the Earth, and the planets going round the Sun. «Isaac Newton explained that gravity makes an apple fall and keeps the Moon going around the Earth.» Gravity is the same pull in both cases, only much bigger out in space.
explain: Gravity is also a word for seriousness. A teacher might say, «Think about the gravity of the situation», meaning how serious it is. Look at the words around it to tell which meaning fits: a force in science, or seriousness in a sentence about a problem.
fact: Gravity is a force that pulls things towards each other.
fact: «The Moon's gravity is about one sixth of the Earth's gravity.» So you would weigh much less on the Moon.
fact: «A ball thrown straight up is pulled by gravity the whole time, even at the top where it seems to stop for a moment.»
fact: Gravity can also mean seriousness, as in «the gravity of the situation» (a formal use).
exercise: «Isaac Newton explained that gravity makes an apple fall and keeps the Moon going around the Earth.» What does gravity do to the apple? | *Pulls it down towards the Earth | Turns it into juice | Makes it glow | Pushes it up into the sky
exercise: «The Moon's gravity is about one sixth of the Earth's gravity.» What does this tell you? | The Moon is one sixth the size of the Earth | Things weigh more on the Moon than on Earth | *Things weigh less on the Moon than on Earth | The Moon has no gravity at all
exercise: Ella's teacher says, «Understand the gravity of the situation.» What does gravity mean here? | A strong wind | *Seriousness, how important and serious something is | A kind of fruit | Feeling sleepy
test: Why do astronauts float in space? | They are too light to weigh anything | They are held up by strong ropes | There is no gravity anywhere in space at all | *They are falling around the Earth all the time
test: «A ball thrown straight up is pulled by gravity the whole time, even at the top where it seems to stop for a moment.» What is gravity doing to the ball at the very top? | Pulling it only sideways | Pulling it only on the way up | Stopping for a moment | *Still pulling it down
exam: «Astronauts in space lose muscle without gravity to work against, so they exercise about two hours a day.» Why do astronauts exercise? | To stop themselves floating away from the spacecraft | To make themselves heavier so they can land | *To stop their muscles getting weaker without gravity to work against | To grow taller in space
